window.docContents[26].resolve({"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Abstract-Minimization":{"contents":"A weakly lower-semicontinuous real functional on a complete real Hilbert\nspace attains a global minimum if the sublevel through some supplied\n is norm bounded:Then there is  such that  for every .\nThis is the compactness-plus-liminf core of the direct method.\n\nThe sublevel through  is weakly closed by lower semicontinuity, weakly\ncompact by boundedness, and nonempty because it contains . The\nfunctional therefore attains a minimum there. That minimum is at most\n, while every point outside the sublevel has larger energy, so the\nconstrained minimum is global.\n\nThe bounded-sublevel hypothesis must be checked at a specific point ;\ncoercivity removes that bookkeeping.\n\nIf  is weakly lower semicontinuous and coercive,then  has a global minimizer.\n\nCoercivity makes the sublevel through the origin norm bounded. Apply the\nbounded-sublevel direct-method theorem to that nonempty sublevel.\n\nBoth existence theorems assume lower semicontinuity for the weak topology, a\nhypothesis that is awkward to verify directly because weakly convergent nets\nneed not converge in norm. Convexity is the standard way to obtain it from the\nnorm topology.\n\nEvery convex real functional on a real normed space that is lower semicontinuous in norm\nis lower semicontinuous for the weak topology:This is the functional-analytic step behind Evans's integral-functional\nargument.\n\nFor every real , the sublevel  is convex because\n is convex and norm closed because  is norm lower semicontinuous. A\nnorm-closed convex set is weakly closed by Hahn–Banach separation. Thus every\nsublevel is weakly closed, which is equivalent to lower semicontinuity of\n in the weak topology.\n\nCombined with the coercive existence theorem, this criterion removes the weak\ntopology from the hypotheses. Completeness of  is still required, since\nthe weak compactness theorem uses it.\n\nA convex functional on a complete real Hilbert space has a global minimizer\nif it is lower semicontinuous in norm and coercive:\n\nConvexity and norm lower semicontinuity give weak lower semicontinuity. The\ncoercive direct method theorem then supplies a minimizer.\n\nConvexity alone leaves room for a whole convex set of minimizers, as a constant\nfunctional shows. Strict convexity excludes this: the midpoint of two distinct\nminimizers would have strictly smaller energy.\n\nStrict convexity upgrades the direct-method conclusion to a unique global\nminimizer:\n\nStrict convexity supplies convexity, so the argument with closed convex sublevel sets\ngives weak lower semicontinuity. The weak direct method gives existence, and\nstrict convexity identifies any two global minimizers.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method","header":"9.3.1.5. Abstract Minimization","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Abstract-Minimization"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Weak-Derivatives-and-Approximation--Weak-Derivatives-and-Sobolev-Spaces":{"contents":"The Sobolev development rests on one analytic relation and two data models,\narranged in three layers:\n\n1. HasWeakDerivativeOn is a proposition about concrete functions;2. SobolevSpaceOn stores one concrete representative together with proofs\n   that suitable derivative representatives exist;3. WkpSpaceOn keeps the base function and all derivative coordinates as\n   Lp classes and is the complete normed space used by operators.\n\nPointwise formulas naturally live at the first two layers, while bounded\nlinear maps, compactness, and variational arguments require the third. The\nbridge between the latter two is canonical from graph space to a selected base\nrepresentative, but choosing all derivative representatives in the reverse\ndirection is noncomputable.\n\nIn the graph model, derivative words are reduced to symmetric\nmulti-indices, completeness holds for every , including\nthe endpoints  and , and equality of Lp classes never\nyields a pointwise equality.\n\nFor a two-dimensional function with two weak derivatives, the graph element\ncan be pictured as\n\n\n\nThese six entries are constrained by the test identities; they cannot be\nchosen independently. The mixed derivative occurs once because the symmetric\nindex identifies the two orders of differentiation. This is the graph in\ngraph space: the compatible tuples of a function and its derivatives.\n\nThe reason this model is complete is visible already in one dimension.\nIf  and  in , pairing with a fixed smooth\ncompactly supported test function passes to the limit:\n\n\n\nHölder's inequality justifies both limits, including the endpoint pairing\nwith a bounded test function when . Thus the limiting derivative\ncoordinate is still the weak derivative of the limiting base coordinate.\nThe closedness proof for the graph space repeats this argument for every\nstored derivative.\n\n\n\nLet , let  be a word of coordinate\nindices, and let .  The predicate\nHasWeakDerivativeOn Ω α f g is defined for an arbitrary set . It\nrequires both functions to be locally integrable on  with respect to\nambient Lebesgue measure and requiresfor every globally smooth compactly supported test function  whose\nclosed support tsupport φ is contained in . Both integrals are\nBochner integrals against the restricted measure\nvolume.restrict Ω; compact support and local integrability make the pairings\nintegrable.Thus the declaration is an arbitrary-set restricted-test relation. When\n is open, these are exactly the usual  tests and\nthe definition has Evans's distributional meaning. On a nonopen set,\nespecially one with empty interior, the available tests can be too small to\ndetermine . PDE-facing predicates such as weak Poisson and weak divergence\nsolutions therefore carry an explicit IsOpen Ω field. The relation says\nnothing about pointwise differentiability of .For open domains, weak derivatives are unique almost everywhere under only\nthe local-integrability hypotheses already present in the predicate.\n\nUniqueness is what later allows a derivative to be stored as a single Lp\nclass rather than as a relation: two functions that both satisfy the identity\nfor the same word on an open set define the same class. The openness\nhypothesis enters here for the first time, and it is inherited by every later\nstatement that identifies a stored coordinate with a distributional\nderivative.\n\nOn an open domain, two proposed derivative representatives satisfying the\nsame weak-derivative identity for the same base function agree almost\neverywhere with respect to volume.restrict Ω. Concretely, ifthen  almost everywhere on . This is Evans's primitive\nuniqueness result. No exponent and no global  hypothesis occur in the\nstatement.\n\nSubtract the two distributional identities and apply the fundamental lemma\nfor locally integrable functions paired against all compactly supported\nsmooth test functions.\n\nUniqueness compares two witnesses for one word. The second basic property of\nthe relation compares one witness across two words. On an open set the two\nresults combine, so witnesses for any two rearrangements of a word agree\nalmost everywhere.\n\nIf two coordinate words are permutations of one another, a weak derivative\nwitness for one word is also a witness for the other. This theorem does not\nrequire  to be open: it follows because every smooth test function has\ncommuting classical mixed derivatives, and a permutation preserves the sign\n.This is the formal reason the completed graph model can use commutative\nmulti-indices rather than one coordinate for every ordered word. It does not\nassume that  itself has classical mixed derivatives.\n\nThe proof first proves equality of the corresponding iterated derivatives of a\nsmooth test function and then rewrites the integration-by-parts identity from\n.\n\nWith the relation and its two basic properties in hand, the first data model\ncan be stated. It adds to a base function exactly the global information that\nthe relation lacks: HasWeakDerivativeOn asks only for local integrability,\nwhereas a Sobolev function needs an  bound on the base function\nand on a witness for every word of length at most .\n\nSobolevSpaceOn n k p Ω contains:* a concrete function ;* a proof that ;* for every coordinate word  with , a proof that\n  some  satisfies\n  HasWeakDerivativeOn Ω α u gα.The derivative clause is existential: it records that derivative\nrepresentatives exist but does not expose a canonical derivative-valued function.\nThat is why it is convenient for hypotheses and pointwise formulas but not\nthe primary normed-space model.Equality of two SobolevSpaceOn values is literal equality of their toFun\nfields, not almost-everywhere equality. The base MemLp field is stated\nseparately. On an open domain it can be recovered from the order-zero\nweak-derivative clause and uniqueness, but on an arbitrary set that\nuniqueness can fail. The type is meaningful for every extended nonnegative\np; its standard Sobolev interpretation uses an open domain and\n.\n\nThe representative model quantifies over ordered words, so \nand  are certified separately even though, by the permutation\ntheorem together with uniqueness, their witnesses agree almost everywhere on\nan open set. A model that stores derivatives as coordinates should not carry\nthis redundancy, and a symmetric index type removes it.\n\nThe graph model does not retain every ordering of a mixed derivative.\nDerivIndex n k is the finite typeimplemented as a dependent pair of an order  and a symmetric power\nof Fin n. Thus  and  use the same coordinate.DerivIndex.toList chooses an ordered representative only when a declaration\nsuch as HasWeakDerivativeOn needs a list. Conversely,\nderivIndexOfList α hα sends a word of length at most  to its symmetric\nindex, and converting it back produces a permutation of , not\nnecessarily the same list. The permutation theorem shows that this choice is\nanalytically harmless.\n\nFixing the index type fixes the shape of a graph element: one \nclass for each symmetric index, the empty index standing for the base\nfunction. The ambient bundle collects these coordinates with no relation\nimposed between them. It is a finite product of Lp spaces, and it is where\nthe norm is defined.\n\nFor , the ambient coordinate bundle isIn Lean this is the finite PiLp product WkpAmbientOn n k p Ω. If\n, its norm is exactlyAt  it is the supremum, hence the maximum, of the finitely many\ncoordinate norms:Some texts instead sum the finitely many  coordinate norms. Those\nnorms are equivalent, but every quantitative PDEAtlas estimate uses the\nPiLp maximum convention unless it explicitly states another norm.\n\nAn ambient family is a Sobolev function only when each coordinate is in fact a\nweak derivative of the base coordinate. The graph space is the subset of the\nbundle cut out by these identities, one for every symmetric index; the ordered\nword that HasWeakDerivativeOn requires is supplied by DerivIndex.toList.\n\nLet  denote the empty multi-index. The predicate IsWkpOn U imposed on\nan ambient family  iswkpSubmoduleOn is the real submodule cut out by these identities, and\nWkpSpaceOn is the same submodule used as a type, for every exponent. The assumption\nFact (1 ≤ p) is not part of the type; where it is available, it gives the finite\nPiLp bundle its normed-space structure. Local integrability of every coordinate\nis a field of the weak-derivative relation itself.Each coordinate is a Mathlib Lp value, so equality is already\nalmost-everywhere equality. On an open domain, uniqueness identifies these\ncoordinates with the usual distributional derivatives of the base\ncoordinate. On an arbitrary set, the type still records the restricted-test\nrelation, and the available tests may be too few to determine the derivative\ncoordinates.\n\nTwo structural operators act on the graph space, and both are contractions\nbecause the bundle norm is a monotone function of the coordinate norms. The\nfirst shrinks the domain while keeping every derivative coordinate.\n\nLet , with  measurable. Coordinatewise\nrestriction of the stored weak derivatives defines a bounded linear mapEvery restricted coordinate agrees almost everywhere on  with\nthe corresponding coordinate on . The map is contractive:Restriction to the same measurable domain is the identity. For\n, with both  and\n measurable,\n\nMonotonicity of restricted Lebesgue measure gives the canonical contractive\nmap on each derivative-coordinate  space. Restricting the test-function\nidentity that defines a weak derivative shows that the coordinate family\nstill satisfies IsWkpOn on the smaller domain. The coordinatewise map\ntherefore restricts to the graph submodule. Monotonicity of the finite\n sum, or of the finite supremum when , gives contraction\nof the graph norm. Identity and composition follow from the\ncorresponding laws for the coordinate  restrictions.\n\nThe second operator keeps the domain and forgets the derivative coordinates of\norder above . The retained identities are unchanged, and omitting\ncoordinates can only decrease the  sum or the maximum, so this map is\nagain contractive; it also commutes with restriction to a measurable\nsubdomain.\n\nIf , forgetting every represented derivative of order greater than\n defines a continuous linear mapEvery retained coordinate is literally the corresponding coordinate of the\noriginal derivative bundle, and forgetting coordinates is contractive:These maps satisfy\n and .\nThey also commute with restriction to measurable subdomains.\nThe canonical first-derivative coordinate  is named uniformly\nat every positive ambient order and is preserved by these order casts.\n\nProject the finite derivative bundle along the canonical inclusion of\nmulti-indices of order at most  into those of order at most .\nThis inclusion is injective, so monotonicity of the finite  sum, or\nof the finite supremum when , gives contraction. The retained\nweak-derivative identities are unchanged. Transitivity, reflexivity, and\ncommutation with domain restriction follow coordinatewise.\n\nBounded operators between normed spaces are only half of what the variational\nand compactness chapters need; they also need limits. Each graph identity is a\nclosed condition on the bundle, so completeness of the finite Lp product\npasses to the graph space for every set  and every .\n\nFor every set  and every , WkpSpaceOn n k p Ω is complete.\nConsequently it is a Banach space at both endpoints  and\n as well as in the reflexive range .At , the ambient PiLp product has the coordinatewise sum inner product,\nthe graph submodule inherits that inner product, and completeness makes\nHkSpaceOn n k Ω = WkpSpaceOn n k 2 Ω a Hilbert space. Openness is needed to\ninterpret the relation as the usual Sobolev space, not for the closed-subspace\nargument itself.\n\nFor each symmetric derivative index  and each test function supported in\n, subtract the two sides of the weak-derivative identity. The result\nis a continuous linear functional on the finite PiLp bundle: its two terms\nare the – pairings with  and , where\n is the Hölder conjugate of . The graph submodule is the intersection\nof the zero sets of all these functionals, hence is closed. A closed subspace\nof the complete finite product of Lp spaces is complete.\n\nAt  the completeness theorem yields a Hilbert space, and the order-one\ncase is the one every variational chapter uses. It is given its own name,\ntogether with the map sending a test function to its classical derivative\nbundle.\n\nFor every  and every set , the Hilbert\nSobolev space used by the variational chapters is the order-one,\nexponent-two graph space:In Lean, H1SpaceOn n Ω is literally WkpSpaceOn n 1 2 Ω. An element is a\nbundle  of  classes in which  is\na weak derivative of  in direction  on , and the norm is\nthe  sum of the coordinate norms. The space is\ncomplete, hence a Hilbert space, and H1Space n is the whole-space case\n.A smooth compactly supported  whose support lies in \ndetermines the element \nwhose derivative coordinates are the classical derivatives;\ntestFunctionToH1 is this map and testFunctionToH1Linear is its linear\nform. When  is open this is Evans's . The definition\nitself imposes no regularity on ; on a nonopen set it retains the\nrestricted-test meaning of the weak-derivative relation.\n\nThe test-function map has range inside , and closing that range\nin the complete space  gives a closed, hence complete, subspace.\nThis closure is the formal replacement for the boundary condition \non ; the trace chapter shows that on an operational \ndomain every element of it has zero trace.\n\nLet  be the test-function map of\n, where  denotes the smooth\ncompactly supported functions with support inside . The zero-trace\nspace is the closure of its range:h10SubmoduleOn is this closed submodule of H1SpaceOn n Ω, and\nH10SpaceOn n Ω is the same submodule used as a normed type with the\nrestricted  norm. It is the case ,  of the general\nsupported-core closure wkp0SubmoduleOn. The linked closure interface\nrecords the defining facts used later:* every test function lies in , and so does every\n   limit of a sequence of test functions;* every  lies in the closure of the test functions,\n  and the canonical map  has dense\n  range;* if  is closed and contains\n  , then .The last item is the closure principle behind every estimate that is proved\nfirst on test functions and then extended to . For open \nthis is Evans's . No boundary regularity enters the\ndefinition.\n\nBoundary conditions on a cube should not require a smooth-boundary trace\ntheorem. The closure definition gives a useful criterion directly.\n\nLet  be open and let \nhave a continuous, compactly supported representative . If\n for every , thenThe boundary may have corners. The hypotheses concern a global Sobolev\nfunction and its representative; continuity alone does not supply its weak\nderivatives.In particular, a globally Lipschitz, compactly supported function that vanishes\noutside  gives an  element directly. Its weak\ncoordinate derivatives agree almost everywhere with its classical partial\nderivatives, even if the function has corners. More generally, compact\nLipschitz functions belong to every  with\n.\n\nFor , clamp the function to a small interval about zero:Subtracting this clamp gives .\nIts support lies in the compact superlevel set\n. Mollifying inside the\npositive collar between this support and the complement of \ntherefore puts  in .As , the removed clamp converges to zero in .\nIts weak derivative is the derivative of  on\n and zero elsewhere. The derivative of \nvanishes almost everywhere on  by the level-set theorem, so\ndominated convergence also sends the derivative error to zero. Thus\n in , and the closedness of\n finishes the proof.For the Lipschitz case, Rademacher's theorem supplies differentiability almost\neverywhere. The derivative is bounded by the Lipschitz constant and is\ncompactly supported, so every derivative row belongs to , including\n. The weak derivative identity gives the global Sobolev element\nto which the zero-exterior criterion applies.\n\nThe representative model and the graph model describe the same functions with\ndifferent data, and the chapter needs to pass between them: pointwise formulas\nare proved on representatives, while operators act on graph elements. In one\ndirection the passage is canonical; in the other it requires choosing\nderivative representatives.\n\ntoRepresentative reads the Lp representative of the base coordinate of a\ngraph-space element and uses the derivative coordinates to certify every\nordered derivative word. Each word is read through its symmetric index, and the\npermutation theorem carries the stored identity back to the word.ofRepresentative goes in the other direction, for exponents at least one. It keeps the given base\nfunction, chooses one  weak-derivative representative for every symmetric\nindex, and sends those functions to their Lp classes. This map is\nnoncomputable because SobolevSpaceOn asserts the existence of derivatives\nrather than selecting canonical ones.\n\nHow much survives a round trip is dictated by two facts. Reading the base\ncoordinate of an Lp class recovers a function only almost everywhere, and\nthe derivative representatives chosen by\nofRepresentative can be identified with the stored coordinates only through\nuniqueness, hence only on an open domain.\n\nThe two round trips have the exact strengths forced by the models.For every set , converting a SobolevSpaceOn value to graph space\nand reading the base coordinate back recovers the original function almost\neverywhere on .If  is open, converting a graph-space element to a representative\nand back is exactly the original graph-space element. Openness is needed:\nwithout it weak derivatives need not be unique, so different choices can\nyield different derivative coordinates.\n\nThe first statement is the defining almost-everywhere property of MemLp.toLp.\nFor the second, compare one symmetric coordinate at a time. The reconstructed\nbase coordinate agrees almost everywhere with the original one. Transport the\nreconstructed weak-derivative identity across that equality, then use\nopen-domain uniqueness to identify the reconstructed derivative with the\noriginal coordinate almost everywhere. Equality in Lp, followed by\nextensionality of the finite product and the graph subtype, gives exact\nequality.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Weak Derivatives and Approximation","header":"4.2.1. Weak Derivatives and Sobolev Spaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Weak-Derivatives-and-Approximation--Weak-Derivatives-and-Sobolev-Spaces"}});